course: dictionary
lesson: 12711
title: Dominoes: tiles, a game, and a chain reaction
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Dominoes has more than one meaning, so the words around it tell you which one you need. «We played dominoes after tea» is about a game, because you play a game.
explain: «Sam lined up twelve dominoes in a long row» is about the small flat tiles, because you line up tiles. Each tile has two halves with spots on them, from 0 to 6. «When the first domino fell, all the others fell too» is about a line of tiles knocked over one after another.
explain: Dominoes is the plural of domino, so one tile is a domino and more than one is dominoes. To check your meaning, swap in «tiles» or «pieces». If the sentence still makes sense, you have found the right meaning.
fact: A domino is a small flat rectangular tile, with two halves marked with spots from 0 to 6.
fact: Dominoes is also the name of the game played with these tiles, where you match the ends.
fact: Dominoes stood up in a row can be knocked over one after another, and «the domino effect» means one event causes the next, and the next.
fact: In maths, dominoes help you count spots, add the two halves and find number patterns.
exercise: «Sam lined up twelve dominoes in a long row.» What are the dominoes in this sentence? | *Small flat tiles with spots | Pieces of a long train | Sweets shaped like bricks | Little wooden horses
exercise: «We played dominoes after tea, and Mum won the first game.» What does dominoes mean here? | Tiny tiles on the floor | A row of toy cars | *The game played with tiles, matching the ends | A kind of pudding
exercise: «One push made the first domino fall, and then the whole line fell.» Which phrase fits this idea? | The bread effect | A set of spots | *The domino effect | Play dominoes
test: «Each domino has two halves, and each half has spots from 0 to 6.» What is the most spots one half can have? | *6 | 12 | 7 | 5
test: «In maths, we use dominoes to add the spots on the two halves.» What do we do in this maths activity? | Eat them | Stack them as high as possible | Paint the tiles | *Count the spots and add the two halves
exam: Which sentence uses dominoes to mean a falling chain reaction? | «We played dominoes in the garden.» | «Count the dominoes in the set.» | «Dominoes are sold in a box.» | *«One domino knocked over the rest, one after another.»
